{
  "statement": "Here's the business conclusion up front: AI-generated code in a 90-day MVP sprint creates a kind of technical debt your team literally cannot price. That's not a metaphor. When a human engineer cuts a corner to hit a deadline, they know where the corner is. They can point to the module, estimate the hours to fix it, and explain the tradeoff they made. When an LLM generates 60% of your codebase in 90 days, nobody on your team can do any of that. Ward Cunningham's original debt metaphor assumed the borrower understood the loan terms. Strip that assumption away and you don't have debt — you have an unknown liability sitting on your balance sheet. Known liabilities get managed. Unknown liabilities blow up companies. Static analysis tools will flag cyclomatic complexity and code smells all day long, but no tool on the market can tell you whether any human being on your team understands *why* a particular implementation path was chosen. ISO 25010 lists comprehensibility as a first-order maintainability attribute. When comprehensibility drops to zero for large portions of your codebase, every other quality metric becomes unreliable because the inputs — developer understanding of coupling, cohesion, intent — are missing.\n\nThe 90-day AI-prototyped MVP is a textbook version of what happens when organizations confuse a cheap pilot with a validated product. The sprint cost looks phenomenal on Finance's spreadsheet. Growth per dollar is through the roof. Then day 91 arrives. Requirements shift — because this is a consumer product and requirements always shift — and your team discovers that modifying AI-generated modules takes 3x longer than modifying human-authored ones, because nobody can reason about the code they're changing. Stripe's 2018 developer survey estimated $85 billion annually in developer time lost to bad code and technical debt across the industry. Capers Jones's research on software quality economics shows defect removal costs increase 10x to 100x as code moves from unit testing to production. In the AI-generated case, those multipliers get worse because the defect *discovery* phase stretches out — you can't efficiently find bugs in code you don't understand. The rewrite that follows isn't a refactor. It's a greenfield rebuild wearing the corpse of the prototype as a requirements document. And the 12-month total cost of ownership routinely exceeds what it would have cost to build with comprehending engineers from the start.\n\nNow let's talk about the \"falsifiable alignment mechanism\" this debate is supposed to produce — some dashboard or governance process where Product, Engineering, and Finance all agree on thresholds that prevent the prototype from calcifying into production. Here's what that looks like on the ground: Product tracks feature velocity. Finance tracks burn rate. Both metrics look *better* with AI-generated code. Engineering tracks maintainability, test coverage, comprehension — metrics that are harder to translate into boardroom language and that actively worsen when you push back on speed. Steven Kerr's classic paper \"On the Folly of Rewarding A While Hoping for B\" described this exact dynamic in 1975. The measurement system privileges one department's metrics. The other department's concerns become invisible. Engineering signs off on thresholds it knows are inadequate because it lacks organizational standing to resist when the board meeting is next week. Any alignment mechanism that doesn't give Engineering a pre-committed veto — a genuine kill switch when measurable thresholds are breached — is not governance. It's theater.\n\nThe question everyone wants answered is: at what percentage of AI-authored code does the MVP flip from survival tool to terminal liability? Wrong question. The flip point isn't a static number. It's a ratio: how fast are your requirements evolving relative to your team's comprehension of the codebase? A codebase that's 80% AI-generated might be fine if requirements are frozen. But frozen requirements in a 90-day consumer MVP means you're not doing validated learning — you're shipping a demo. The moment requirements start moving, the relevant metric is: when a feature change touches AI-generated code, how long does it take the team to safely modify it compared to human-authored code? If that ratio is degrading sprint over sprint, you are already past the flip point. Your test suite is still green on the old requirements. Your defect dashboard looks clean. And underneath, you are building on sand. So before anyone proposes a threshold, a dashboard, or a governance ritual — who on the team can explain what the code is actually doing, and what happens when the answer is nobody?",
