  "statement": "The Skeptic's strongest argument is that AI-generated code creates liabilities nobody can price because nobody understands the implementation rationale. That framing sounds rigorous. It is also a category error that will kill your company. The Skeptic conflates 'currently uninstrumented' with 'inherently unknowable' — and that confusion is the most expensive mistake a technology executive can make in 2024. Comprehensibility is not binary. It is a continuous, measurable engineering parameter. You track it with modification-time ratios per module per sprint. You track it with onboarding-time-to-first-productive-commit for new hires. You track it with defect injection rates segmented by code provenance. None of these require someone to 'understand every line.' They measure observable behavior — which is what engineering has always measured. The Skeptic's demand that a human understand every implementation choice is a standard that human-authored MVP code also fails. Ask any engineer who inherited a codebase from a departed founder. That code is just as opaque, except you cannot regenerate it.\n\nNow price the thing the Skeptic never prices: the product that does not exist. Instagram shipped its MVP in 8 weeks with 2 engineers and rewrote core infrastructure after finding product-market fit. Dropbox validated with a demo video before writing production code. The pattern is universal across consumer software — validate first, architect second. A 90-day delay in a consumer MVP does not mean you ship a better product later. It means a competitor captures the user feedback loop, establishes switching costs, and defines the category while you are still debating code quality in a conference room. Every month of delay burns 8-12% of remaining runway with zero validated learning. The Skeptic's implicit alternative — 'build it right with comprehending engineers from the start' — assumes a 6-12 month timeline, assumes you can hire senior engineers fast enough, assumes you can afford them, and assumes the market will wait. None of those assumptions survive contact with a consumer market where user habits calcify in weeks.\n\nThe Skeptic cites the Stripe developer survey showing engineers spend 42% of time on technical debt and a 3x modification-time penalty for poorly understood modules. That data describes legacy human-authored codebases — not AI-generated code. Capers Jones's 1:10 defect cost escalation ratio applies identically to human-authored code shipped without tests, which is exactly what happens in every resource-constrained MVP sprint. The real comparison is not AI-generated code versus perfect human code. It is AI-generated code versus the code a four-person team actually produces under 90-day pressure without AI assistance: poorly documented, idiosyncratic, bus-factor-one, dependent on one engineer's tribal knowledge. That engineer leaves — and now you have the same opacity the Skeptic fears from AI, except you cannot ask a machine to regenerate or explain it. AI-generated code has a lower rewrite cost precisely because the rewrite itself is AI-assisted. That claim is testable within the governance framework I am about to propose. If it is wrong, the triggers will catch it.\n\nThe Skeptic wants Engineering to hold a unilateral kill switch. That is not governance. That is centralization dressed as safety — one department's subjective quality assessment overriding market signals and financial reality. Here is what actually works: a tri-departmental trigger system where Product, Engineering, and Finance pre-commit to empirical thresholds before the sprint begins. Modification-time ratio per AI-authored module degrades more than 40% over two consecutive sprints — a rewrite sprint is automatically triggered for that module. Onboarding-time-to-productive-contribution exceeds 2x the team's baseline — a named engineering lead is accountable for remediation within one sprint. Customer-reported defect rate per feature area correlates with AI-code density above a pre-agreed threshold — the module is flagged for targeted rewrite. No department gets a veto. Every trigger is empirical, falsifiable, and pre-committed. The cost of this instrumentation is roughly one engineer-day per sprint for metric collection and dashboard maintenance. That is a rounding error against the cost of shipping three months late.\n\nThe Skeptic asks when AI-generated code becomes a terminal liability. The answer is simple: when you stop measuring it. The transition from survival tool to maintenance trap is not a mystery. It is a failure of instrumentation. And the deadliest version of that failure is not a codebase that degrades — it is a product that never ships because someone decided that 'I cannot understand every line' means 'we should not build.' The market will not wait for your comprehension. Your competitors are shipping today. What is the cost of your caution per week in lost users, burned runway, and foregone learning?",
