assisted. Think about what that means operationally. You're using the same tool that created the incomprehensible code to fix the incomprehensible code. Each rewrite adds another layer nobody fully understands. The modification-time ratio may look stable precisely because the AI is papering over problems — the metric improves while actual comprehension degrades. This is a debt spiral. Historical parallel: pre-2008 risk models where Value-at-Risk metrics looked fine because the models generating the metrics were built on the same assumptions as the instruments they were measuring. The dashboard was green until it wasn't. For a product executive, the operational risk is this: you ship, you get traction, you need to pivot based on user data at week eight, and your team discovers that three layers of AI-assisted rewrites have created a codebase where nobody — human or machine — can predict the second-order effects of changing the payment flow.\n\nThe opportunity-cost argument has real teeth. Burning 8-12% of remaining runway per month with zero validated learning is genuinely dangerous. Nobody serious is arguing \"don't ship.\" The veto isn't a delay mechanism. It's a circuit breaker. It doesn't prevent launching the MVP. It prevents the specific failure mode where degradation accumulates past the point of recovery while three departments are still negotiating whether the 40% threshold was actually breached or whether the baseline shifted. As described in \"Intelligent AI Delegation,\" long delegation chains create an accountability vacuum where failure can't be attributed to a single node — and a tri-departmental trigger system with no halt authority is exactly that kind of chain. Someone has to be able to pull the emergency brake without a committee vote. The question the Accelerationist hasn't answered: when the metrics themselves are generated by the same tool they're supposed to measure, who in that tri-departmental system has the authority to say \"these numbers are lying to us\" — and what happens if nobody does?",
