letes it. Wire the leading indicators into the denominator as a real-time comprehension signal. Wire product-backlog velocity (stories added, stories changed, stories deleted per sprint) into the numerator. The threshold isn't a fixed 40% wall anymore. It's a rolling 3-sprint baseline with the circuit breaker firing at 2 standard deviations from the rolling mean of that ratio. Recalibration happens automatically every sprint via the CI pipeline — no committee, no three-department argument, no human bottleneck. The dashboard updates itself. If the ratio spikes because Product added 15 new stories while comprehension probe scores dropped 20%, the breaker fires before the next sprint planning meeting. This is cheaper than one engineer-day per sprint to maintain because the entropy and dead-code checks are static-analysis jobs that already run in most CI pipelines. The comprehension probes cost maybe two hours of engineer time per week. That's the operational burden. Compare it to the cost of a rewrite at day 75.\n\nThe 2008 VaR analogy is vivid but structurally wrong. VaR failed because the same institutions rated their own instruments — Moody's grading CDOs it helped structure. AI-assisted rewrites are categorically different. The human engineer specifies the target: reduce cyclomatic complexity below 10, enforce the team style guide, add inline documentation at every branch point. The AI executes. The output is then validated by the same independent leading indicators — entropy, comprehension probes, dead-code ratios — applied to all code, AI-generated or not. The measurement tool is independent of the rewrite tool. That's closer to an external auditor than to Moody's marking its own homework. GitHub's internal studies on Copilot-assisted refactoring show 20-30% reductions in cyclomatic complexity when engineers direct the tool to simplify. The AI isn't grading itself. The engineer is directing the AI, and the CI pipeline is grading the result.\n\nNow flip that 70-80