ers influenced by AI outputs lose critical judgment over time, and the research on how to prevent that is still missing.\n\nOn the rolling baseline: the 3-sprint window with a 2-standard-deviation breaker sounds disciplined. But a rolling baseline that recalibrates against its own recent history can normalize slow degradation — the boiling frog. Here's an observable test that works inside 90 days. Freeze one representative module at sprint two. Never let AI touch it. Every sprint, measure the time a randomly selected engineer takes to implement a small, novel requirement change in that frozen canary module versus in AI-generated modules of comparable size. If the canary's modification time stays flat at, say, 4 hours while AI-module modification time drifts from 4 hours to 6 hours to 8 hours — even within the rolling baseline's 2-sigma band — the baseline is masking decline. The canary is the external anchor. It costs you one frozen module and about 3 engineer-hours per sprint to run the test. That's the cheapest insurance policy in the entire framework. Long delegation chains without a fixed reference point create what the research calls an accountability vacuum where failure attribution becomes impossible — and a rolling baseline without a canary is exactly that kind of chain.\n\nOn the VaR analogy: the narrow point is right. Static analysis tools are technically independent of the AI code generator. Conceded. But tool-level independence is not the same as workflow-level independence. The engineer directing the rewrite cycle relies on AI suggestions for what to fix. The metrics measuring success are structural — entropy, dead code, probe scores. The engineer's own cross-module reasoning capacity may be degrading through repeated AI reliance, the same pattern the Brookings study documented in \"It was never the keyboard\" where low-performing users saw an 8% decline when given AI tools. The entire feedback loop — generate, measure, rewrite, re-measure — can be inte