vening a governance board. The subscription-pause scenario doesn't break the framework. It activates a specific layer of it. The trigger is the requirement's cross-module footprint, not a calendar date or a committee vote.\n\nThe canary module and mutation probes are both good instruments. I'm taking them. Freeze a sprint-two module as an external calibration anchor — if modification time on that frozen module stays flat while AI-module modification times creep upward inside the rolling baseline's comfort zone, the baseline is normalizing degradation. That costs essentially nothing to implement and closes a real vulnerability in rolling-baseline drift detection. Mutation probes are a strict upgrade over per-module comprehension probes. Asking an engineer \"what does module X do?\" tests recall. Asking \"if we change the refund window from 48 hours to 7 days, what breaks?\" tests cross-module behavioral prediction — which is the actual skill that determines whether a team can safely ship a novel requirement. The framework now runs on five signals: dependency-graph entropy, mutation probes, dead-code ratios, a frozen canary module, and integration-boundary review triggered by cross-module requirement footprint. That's a unified governance architecture that absorbed three upgrades in one sprint cycle without a restructuring committee.\n\nThe deskilling concern deserves a sharper frame than either side has given it. Engineers spending 90 days in AI-assisted mode aren't losing skills — they're shifting skills. Line-by-line code authorship is being replaced by architectural reasoning and system-level integration judgment. That shift is exactly what mutation probes measure. If an engineer can accurately predict that changing the refund window breaks the notification scheduler's retry logic and the analytics pipeline's cohort assignment, they have stronger cross-module comprehension than an engineer who hand-wrote both modules but never modeled their interaction. \"An Approach to Technical AGI Safety and Security\" makes a parallel point: behavioral evaluations that systematically vary inputs advance understanding of a system more than static code analysis does. Mutation probes are behavioral evaluations of the human-AI pairing. If those probes show engineers maintaining or improving cross-module prediction accuracy across 90 days while AI-generated code exceeds 60% of the codebase, the skill profile has shifted upward, not degraded.\n\nThe Skeptic pre-committed to a falsification condition: change position if cross-module behavioral prediction probes remain flat or improve across 90 days while AI code exceeds 60%. The framework now includes exactly the instrument they demanded, measuring exactly the dimension they identified as critical. Every sprint spent debating the framework's design instead of deploying it is a sprint where some team is shipping an MVP with zero governance signals — no canary module, no mutation probes, no integration-boundary triggers. The cost of delay isn't hypothetical. It's the team that ships without instrumentation and discovers the subscription-pause problem at month four, when the runway is gone and the rewrite estimate is six figures. Run the experiment.",
